From: evergo <evergo@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH 0087e] evergo: read-only root with FSK lastMountedVersion: skip journal in late init too

Our root volume has lastMountedVersion 'fsck', so the mount takes
hfs_late_journal_init() instead of hfs_early_journal_init(); its
read-only gate had the same MNT_ROOTFS exclusion, and the write
attempt against the read-only UFS device failed the root mount
with EINVAL (22).  Mirror 0086e: drop the exclusion and continue
without a journal when the journal is dirty.
---
diff --git a/bsd/sweet_hfs/core/hfs_vfsutils.c b/bsd/sweet_hfs/core/hfs_vfsutils.c
index 9556c95..954b22e 100644
--- a/bsd/sweet_hfs/core/hfs_vfsutils.c
+++ b/bsd/sweet_hfs/core/hfs_vfsutils.c
@@ -2827,10 +2827,18 @@ hfs_late_journal_init(struct hfsmount *hfsmp, HFSPlusVolumeHeader *vhp, void *_a
 	hfsmp->jnl_start = jib_offset / SWAP_BE32(vhp->blockSize);
 	hfsmp->jnl_size  = jib_size;
 
-	if ((hfsmp->hfs_flags & HFS_READ_ONLY) && (vfs_flags(hfsmp->hfs_mp) & MNT_ROOTFS) == 0) {
-	    // if the file system is read-only, check if the journal is empty.
-	    // if it is, then we can allow the mount.  otherwise we have to
-	    // return failure.
+	if (hfsmp->hfs_flags & HFS_READ_ONLY) {
+	    /*
+	     * evergo (0087e): same story as 0086e but in the late journal init
+	     * path.  A volume with lastMountedVersion == FSK skips
+	     * hfs_early_journal_init entirely and lands here, where the
+	     * MNT_ROOTFS exclusion again sent our read-only root into
+	     * journal_create()/journal_open(JOURNAL_RESET), which wrote to the
+	     * read-only UFS device (do_jnl_io: strategy err 0x1e) and failed
+	     * the root mount with EINVAL.  Treat the root like any other
+	     * read-only volume, and when the journal is dirty simply mount
+	     * without a journal instead of failing.
+	     */
 	    retval = journal_is_clean(hfsmp->jvp,
 				      jib_offset,
 				      jib_size,
@@ -2843,9 +2851,10 @@ hfs_late_journal_init(struct hfsmount *hfsmp, HFSPlusVolumeHeader *vhp, void *_a
 
 	    if (retval) {
 		    const char *name = vnode_getname_printable(devvp);
-		    printf("hfs: late journal init: volume on %s is read-only and journal is dirty.  Can not mount volume.\n", 
+		    printf("hfs: late journal init: %s is read-only with a dirty journal; continuing without one\n",
 		    name);
 		    vnode_putname_printable(name);
+		    retval = 0;
 	    }
 
 	    return retval;
